Request parameters <p>The following parameters are available for the Veo model. For more information, see Generate videos using text and image prompts using Veo.</p> Parameter Type Description <code>prompt</code> <code>string</code> Required for text-to-video. A text string to guide video generation. Examples: <code>A fast-tracking shot through a bustling dystopian sprawl with bright neon signs</code>, <code>Timelapse of the northern lights dancing across the Arctic sky</code>. <code>image</code> <code>object</code> Required for image-to-video. An input image for guiding video generation. Provide either <code>bytesBase64Encoded</code> or <code>gcsUri</code>. Recommended resolution is 1280x720 or 720x1280. <code>durationSeconds</code> <code>integer</code> Required. The length of the video to generate in seconds.  • <code>veo-2.0-generate-001</code>: <code>5</code>-<code>8</code> (default: <code>8</code>).  • <code>veo-3.0-generate-preview</code>: <code>8</code>. <code>storageUri</code> <code>string</code> Optional. A Cloud Storage URI to store the output video (e.g., <code>gs://BUCKET_NAME/SUBDIRECTORY</code>). If not provided, base64-encoded video bytes are returned. <code>sampleCount</code> <code>integer</code> Optional. The number of videos to generate. Accepted values: <code>1</code>-<code>4</code>. <code>aspectRatio</code> <code>string</code> Optional. The aspect ratio of the generated video.  • <code>16:9</code> (default, landscape)  • <code>9:16</code> (portrait, not supported by <code>veo-3.0-generate-preview</code>) <code>negativePrompt</code> <code>string</code> Optional. A text string describing what to avoid in the video. Examples: <code>overhead lighting, bright colors</code>, <code>people, animals</code>. <code>personGeneration</code> <code>string</code> Optional. Safety setting for generating people.  • <code>allow_adult</code> (default): Allow generation of adults.  • <code>dont_allow</code>: Disallow generation of people/faces. <code>seed</code> <code>uint32</code> Optional. A number from <code>0</code> to <code>4,294,967,295</code> to make video generation deterministic. Using the same seed and parameters produces the same videos. <code>enhancePrompt</code> <code>boolean</code> Optional. If <code>true</code> (default), Gemini is used to enhance your prompt. <code>generateAudio</code> <code>boolean</code> Required for <code>veo-3.0-generate-preview</code>. If <code>true</code>, generates audio for the video. Not supported by <code>veo-2.0-generate-001</code>. Response body <p> Generate video request response</p> <p>The initial request to generate a video returns the following response, which contains the name of the long-running operation.</p> <pre><code>{
  "name": string
}
</code></pre> Element Description <code>name</code> The full operation name of the long-running operation. <p> Poll operation response</p> <p>Polling the status of the long-running operation returns the following response. When <code>done</code> is <code>true</code>, the <code>response</code> field contains the results.</p> <pre><code>{
   "name": string,
   "done": boolean,
   "response":{
      "@type":"type.googleapis.com/cloud.ai.large_models.vision.GenerateVideoResponse",
      "generatedSamples":[
         {
            "video":
            {
               "uri": string,
               "encoding": string
            }
         }
      ]
   }
}
</code></pre> Element Description <code>name</code> The full operation name. <code>done</code> A boolean that is <code>true</code> when the operation is complete. <code>response</code> The response body of the completed operation. <code>generatedSamples</code> An array of the generated video sample objects. <code>video.uri</code> The Cloud Storage URI of the generated video. <code>video.encoding</code> The video encoding type (e.g., <code>video/mp4</code>).

Steps for cleanup <p>To avoid ongoing charges, delete the resources created during this tutorial.</p> <p>1. Delete endpoints and models</p> <p>This code undeploys each model and deletes the associated endpoints and model resources.</p> <pre><code># Undeploy model and delete endpoint
for endpoint in endpoints.values():
    endpoint.delete(force=True)

# Delete models
for model in models.values():
    model.delete()
</code></pre> <p>2. (Optional) Delete the Cloud Storage bucket</p> <p>If you created a new Cloud Storage bucket for this tutorial, you can delete it.</p> <pre><code>delete_bucket = False
if delete_bucket:
    ! gsutil -m rm -r $BUCKET_NAME
</code></pre> <p></p>

Google Cloud consoleVertex AI API <p>You can enable the layout parser when creating a new corpus in the Google Cloud console.</p> Steps to import files using the Google Cloud console <ol> <li> <p>In the Google Cloud console, go to the RAG Engine page.</p> <p>Go to RAG Engine</p> </li> <li> <p>Select Create corpus.</p> </li> <li>In the Region field, select your region.</li> <li>In the Corpus name field, enter a name for your corpus.</li> <li>In the Description field, enter a description.</li> <li>In the Data section, select the source of your data.</li> <li>Expand the Advanced options section.<ul> <li>In the Chunking strategy section, the following default sizes are recommended:<ul> <li>Chunking size: 1024</li> <li>Chunk overlap: 256</li> </ul> </li> <li>In the Layout parser section, select the LLM parser option.<ol> <li>From the Model field, select your model.</li> <li>Optional: In the Maximum parsing requests per min field, enter your maximum parsing requests.</li> <li>Optional: In the Custom parsing prompt field, enter your parsing prompt.</li> <li>Click Continue.</li> </ol> </li> </ul> </li> <li>On the Configure vector store page, do the following:<ol> <li>In the Embedding model field, select your embedding model.</li> <li>In the Vector database section, select your database.</li> </ol> </li> <li>Click Create corpus.</li> </ol> <p>You can use the <code>ImportRagFiles</code> method in the Vertex AI API to import files and apply the layout parser programmatically.</p> <p>Troubleshooting</p> <p>If you encounter issues with code snippets or setup, refer to the Troubleshooting Guide.</p> PythonREST <p>To learn how to install or update the Vertex AI SDK for Python, see Install the Vertex AI SDK for Python. For more information, see the Python API reference documentation.</p> <p>Before running the sample, replace the following variables: *   <code>PROJECT_ID</code>: Your project ID. *   <code>LOCATION</code>: The region to process the request (for example, <code>us-central1</code>). *   <code>RAG_CORPUS_ID</code>: The ID of your RAG corpus. *   <code>GCS_URIS</code>: A list of Cloud Storage URIs (for example, <code>["gs://my-bucket1", "gs://my-bucket2"]</code>). *   <code>LAYOUT_PARSER_PROCESSOR_NAME</code>: The full resource name of your layout parser processor (for example, <code>"projects/{project}/locations/{location}/processors/{processor_id}"</code>).</p> <pre><code>from vertexai import rag
import vertexai

# TODO(developer):- Update these variables before running the sample.
PROJECT_ID = "your-project-id"
LOCATION = "us-central1"
RAG_CORPUS_ID = "your-rag-corpus-id"
GCS_URIS = ["gs://your-bucket/your-files-dir"]
LAYOUT_PARSER_PROCESSOR_NAME = "projects/your-project-id/locations/us-central1/processors/your-processor-id"

# Initialize Vertex AI API
vertexai.init(project=PROJECT_ID, location=LOCATION)

response = rag.import_files(
    corpus_name=f"projects/{PROJECT_ID}/locations/{LOCATION}/ragCorpora/{RAG_CORPUS_ID}",
    paths=GCS_URIS,
    chunk_size=1024,  # Optional
    chunk_overlap=256,  # Optional
    parsing_config=rag.ParsingConfig(
        layout_parser=rag.LayoutParser(processor_name=LAYOUT_PARSER_PROCESSOR_NAME)
    ),
)
print(f"Imported {response.imported_rag_files_count} files.")
</code></pre> <p>The following sample shows how to import Cloud Storage files using the layout parser. For more configuration options, see the <code>ImportRagFilesConfig</code> reference.</p> <p>Before using the request data, replace the following variables: *   <code>PROJECT_ID</code>: Your project ID. *   <code>LOCATION</code>: The region to process the request (for example, <code>us-central1</code>). *   <code>RAG_CORPUS_ID</code>: The ID of your RAG corpus. *   <code>GCS_URIS</code>: A list of Cloud Storage URIs (for example, <code>["gs://my-bucket1", "gs://my-bucket2"]</code>). *   <code>LAYOUT_PARSER_PROCESSOR_NAME</code>: The full resource name of your layout parser processor (for example, <code>"projects/{project}/locations/{location}/processors/{processor_id}"</code>). *   <code>CHUNK_SIZE</code>: The number of tokens each chunk should have (for example, <code>1024</code>).</p> <p>HTTP request: <pre><code>POST https://LOCATION-aiplatform.googleapis.com/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/locations/LOCATION/ragCorpora/RAG_CORPUS_ID/ragFiles:import
</code></pre></p> <p>Request JSON body: <pre><code>{
  "importRagFilesConfig": {
    "gcsSource": {
      "uris": GCS_URIS
    },
    "ragFileParsingConfig": {
      "layoutParser": {
        "processorName": "LAYOUT_PARSER_PROCESSOR_NAME"
      }
    },
    "ragFileTransformationConfig": {
      "ragFileChunkingConfig": {
        "fixedLengthChunking": {
          "chunkSize": CHUNK_SIZE
        }
      }
    },
  }
}
</code></pre></p> <p>To send your request, choose one of the following options:</p> <p>==== "cURL"     Note: The following command assumes that you have signed in to the gcloud CLI with your user account by running <code>gcloud init</code> or <code>gcloud auth login</code>, or by using Cloud Shell, which automatically signs you into the gcloud CLI. You can check the active account by running <code>gcloud auth list</code>.</p> <pre><code>Save the request body in a file named `request.json`, and execute the following command:

```bash
curl -X POST \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $(gcloud auth print-access-token)" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8" \
    -d @request.json \
    "https://LOCATION-aiplatform.googleapis.com/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/locations/LOCATION/ragCorpora/RAG_CORPUS_ID/ragFiles:import"
```
</code></pre> <p>==== "PowerShell"     Note: The following command assumes that you have signed in to the gcloud CLI with your user account by running <code>gcloud init</code> or <code>gcloud auth login</code>. You can check the active account by running <code>gcloud auth list</code>.</p> <pre><code>Save the request body in a file named `request.json`, and execute the following command:

```powershell
$cred = gcloud auth print-access-token
$headers = @{ "Authorization" = "Bearer $cred" }

Invoke-WebRequest `
    -Method POST `
    -Headers $headers `
    -ContentType: "application/json; charset=utf-8" `
    -InFile request.json `
    -Uri "https://LOCATION-aiplatform.googleapis.com/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/locations/LOCATION/ragCorpora/RAG_CORPUS_ID/ragFiles:import" | Select-Object -Expand Content
```
</code></pre>

